                <pb facs="0365" n="221" sameAs="frus:frus1929v02/pg221"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77 Chinese Eastern/60: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Harbin</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Hanson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Harbin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-07-21T20:00:00+08:30">July 21, 1929—8
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received July 22—12:32 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>Report received to the effect that two Chinese merchant ships <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Ilan</hi> [and] <hi rendition="#italic">Haicheng</hi>
                    seized by Soviet Russians on Amur River who have assured Roy Talbot, American
                    Commissioner of Customs, Taheiho, that they will treat kindly his wife and child
                    who are on <hi rendition="#italic">Ilan</hi>. No new developments except all
                    Soviet Russian employees of the railway have been requested to resign their
                    positions by order of the Soviet Government. Legation informed.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hanson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
